#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:5
msgid "Events"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:6
msgid "What's the best way to keep informed in xonsh? Subscribe to an event!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:9
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:10
msgid ""
"Simply, events are a way for various pieces of xonsh to tell each other "
"what's going on. They're fired when something of note happens, eg the "
"current directory changes or just before a command is executed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:14
msgid "See the `event list <events.html>`_ for the core xonsh events."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:17
msgid "Show me the code!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:19
msgid ""
"This will add a line to a file every time the current directory changes (due "
"to ``cd``, ``pushd``, or several other commands):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:22
msgid ""
"@events.on_chdir\n"
"def _add_to_file(olddir, newdir, **kw):\n"
"    with open('/tmp/.dirhist', 'a') as dh:\n"
"        print(newdir, file=dh)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:30
msgid ""
"Note that the event system is keyword only. Event handlers must match "
"argument names and must have a ``**kw`` as protection against future changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:34
msgid "Can I use this in my xontrib?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:36
msgid ""
"Yes! It's even easy! In your xontrib, you just have to do something like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:38
msgid ""
"events.doc('myxontrib_on_spam', \"\"\"\n"
"myxontrib_on_spam(can: Spam) -> bool?\n"
"\n"
"Fired in case of spam. Return ``True`` if it's been eaten.\n"
"\"\"\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:46
msgid ""
"This will enable users to call ``help(events.myxontrib_on_spam)`` and get "
"useful output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:49
msgid "Listing Registered Handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:51
msgid ""
"You can easily inspect which handlers are registered for each event by "
"simply printing the ``events`` object. This is useful for debugging and "
"understanding which xontribs are active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:55
msgid ""
"@events.on_precommand\n"
"def my_handler(cmd, **_):\n"
"    pass\n"
"\n"
"print(events)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:63
msgid "This will produce output similar to the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:65
msgid ""
"on_lscolors_change:\n"
"  - xonsh.pyghooks.on_lscolors_change\n"
"on_pre_spec_run_ls:\n"
"  - xonsh.environ.ensure_ls_colors_in_env\n"
"on_precommand:\n"
"  - __main__.my_handler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:74
msgid ""
"To get this information programmatically, you can use the ``events."
"handlers()`` method, which returns a dictionary mapping event names to a "
"list of handler strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:77
msgid ""
"events.handlers()\n"
"# {'on_lscolors_change': ['xonsh.pyghooks.on_lscolors_change'], ...}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:83
msgid "Notes for developers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:85
msgid "Handler call order is not guaranteed now."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:87
msgid ""
"``Event.fire()`` returns a list of the returns from the handlers. You should "
"merge this list if it's needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../events_tutorial.rst:89
msgid ""
"In xonsh, events come in species. Each one may look like an event and quack "
"like an event, but they behave differently. This was done because load hooks "
"look like events and quack like events, but they have different semantics. "
"See `LoadEvents <api/events.html#xonsh.events.LoadEvent>`_ for details. In "
"order to turn an event from the default ``Event``, you must transmogrify it, "
"using ``events.transmogrify()``. The class the event is turned in to must be "
"a subclass of ``AbstractEvent``. Under the hood, transmogrify creates a new "
"instance and copies the handlers and docstring from the old instance to the "
"new one."
msgstr ""

